    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `collect_dataclass_fields`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `collect_dataclass_fields` function is a core component in Pydantic's internal machinery for processing dataclasses. It aggregates and processes field information from a given dataclass, including annotations, default values, and other metadata, into a dictionary of `FieldInfo` objects. This function is essential for Pydantic to understand and validate the structure of dataclasses effectively.\n\nThe function accepts several parameters: `cls`, which is the dataclass type to be processed; `ns_resolver`, an optional namespace resolver for handling type evaluation; `typevars_map`, an optional dictionary for mapping type variables; and `config_wrapper`, an optional configuration wrapper for additional settings. If `ns_resolver` is not provided, it defaults to a new instance of `NsResolver`.\n\nThe function iterates over the method resolution order (MRO) of the class in reverse, ensuring that base class fields are processed before derived class fields. It skips non-dataclass bases and evaluates field types using the `ns_resolver`. It also checks for `ClassVar` annotations and skips fields that are not initialized or have conflicting `init` and `init_var` settings.\n\nIf a field's default value is a `FieldInfo` object, the function ensures that the `init` and `init_var` settings are consistent. It then constructs a `FieldInfo` object for each field, applying any type variable mappings and updating fields from docstrings if a `config_wrapper` is provided.\n\nThe function returns a dictionary mapping field names to their corresponding `FieldInfo` objects. It relies on several helper functions and methods, such as `import_cached_field_info`, `_typing_extra.eval_type`, and `_update_fields_from_docstrings`, to perform its tasks. The function handles errors by raising a `PydanticUserError` if there are conflicting `init` and `init_var` settings for a field.\n\nOne side effect of this function is that it may modify the class attributes if a field's default value is a `FieldInfo` object, ensuring that the default is correctly set.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
